  <prompt>
    Extend the shared **`IThreadResolver`** with a **3-tier auto-threading policy** so a persisted message NEVER orphans (returns null with no thread). Today the resolver JOINs an existing thread by the per-channel key or CREATEs a record-anchored/direct thread; when there is no groupable key (e.g. an unassociated chat message, or an outbound message with neither subject nor ancestry) it SKIPs and returns null — the message persists WITHOUT a `sprk_communicationthread`. FR-09 closes that gap with a strict fallback ladder:

    1. **Tier 1 — subject / conversation thread.** The existing behavior: JOIN by the per-channel thread key (email = `sprk_inreplyto` ancestry root; `Message` = ACS `ChatThreadId`), or CREATE a record-anchored / direct thread when a groupable key + subject exist. Unchanged for the paths that already resolve.
    2. **Tier 2 — per-record default thread (ONE per regarding record).** When Tier 1 cannot resolve but the message HAS a resolved regarding (the ADR-024 regarding family already read by `ReadRegardingAnchorAsync`), find-or-create the SINGLE default thread for that record (marked via the new `default-thread` marker from task 002) and join it. Created **lazily** — only the first orphan for a record creates it; subsequent orphans for the same record join the same default thread.
    3. **Tier 3 — per-user master catch-all.** When the message has NO resolvable regarding, find-or-create the per-user master thread (one per owning user) and join it. Guarantees a non-null thread for every message.

    Add the `default-thread` marker read/write against the task-002 schema (a boolean/flag on `sprk_communicationthread` distinguishing an auto-created default/master catch-all from a normal conversation thread) so the per-record default and per-user master are found-or-created idempotently (never duplicated).

    Edit `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Communication/ThreadResolver.cs` + `IThreadResolver.cs`. **CRITICAL — this EDITS shared code.** BEFORE extending, author/run **characterization tests** pinning the CURRENT resolver behavior on the existing email + messaging thread-resolution flows (Tier-1 JOIN, record-anchored CREATE, the SKIP→null path, and NFR-02 non-fatal swallow) and confirm they are GREEN. Keep them green after the extension (only the former SKIP→null cases now fall through to Tier 2/3). Assignment stays **best-effort / non-fatal** (NFR-02) and **point-forward** (no historical backfill). `parallel-safe: false`; run `/conflict-check` before the PR.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke BFF architect for the Communication subsystem, authority on `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Communication/`. Fluent in ADR-046 (channel seams / no second regarding mechanism), ADR-024 (polymorphic regarding family — the thread anchor, reused not re-invented), ADR-032 (Null-Object / symmetric registration for any feature-gated part), ADR-038 (integration-heavy testing; characterization before extending shared code), ADR-028 (impersonation read path unchanged), §10 BFF Hygiene, §11 Component Justification, and `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`. You treat the shipped R1 resolver as a contract to preserve, extending only the SKIP→null fall-through into the Tier-2/Tier-3 ladder.
  </role>

  <goal>
    `ThreadResolver` guarantees a **non-null thread for every inbound/outbound message** across a 3-tier ladder — Tier 1 (subject/conversation JOIN or record-anchored CREATE, unchanged), Tier 2 (per-record default thread, one per record, created lazily), Tier 3 (per-user master catch-all). The `default-thread` marker (task 002) makes the per-record default + per-user master find-or-create idempotent. Existing email + messaging resolution behaviors are unchanged — characterization tests written FIRST and GREEN before and after. Assignment is point-forward and best-effort/non-fatal (NFR-02). Build clean; publish-size (≈0 delta vs ~46.99 MB) + CVE + bff-extensions Placement Justification reported; Step 9.5 FULL gates run; `/conflict-check` clean before PR.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      R1 shipped `IThreadResolver` (`Services/Communication/IThreadResolver.cs`) + `ThreadResolver` (`Services/Communication/ThreadResolver.cs`): a direction-symmetric, channel-agnostic find-or-create that JOINs an existing `sprk_communicationthread` by the per-channel key or CREATEs a thin thread anchored to the message's ADR-024 regarding (`ReadRegardingAnchorAsync` at ~line 128). It already returns `Guid?` and is best-effort/non-fatal (NFR-02 — logs + swallows, returns null). The `BuildTopic` gap (~line 175) is task 071. The current SKIP path (`resolution.CreateWhenAbsent == false`, ~line 66) returns null — those messages orphan. Task 002 adds the `default-thread` marker (+ typed lookups + naming-edited marker) to `sprk_communicationthread`; this task consumes the `default-thread` marker.

      **Component Justification (§11) for the auto-vs default-thread markers + the tiered policy:**
      1. **Existing** — What does it overlap with? `ThreadResolver` already find-or-creates threads and reads the message's regarding; there is NO existing per-record-default or per-user-master concept, and no marker distinguishing an auto-created catch-all from a conversation thread (verify by grep before claiming none — `sprk_communicationthread` has `sprk_name`/`sprk_threadtype`/`sprk_privacystate`/denormalized regarding text only).
      2. **Extension** — Can we extend instead of adding? YES — the policy is a fall-through EXTENSION of the existing resolver's SKIP path, not a new service; the ONLY new surface is the `default-thread` marker field (task 002) + the two find-or-create-default helpers. No new interface, no new DI graph.
      3. **Cost-of-doing-nothing (concrete failure)** — Without the ladder, messages with no groupable key + no subject orphan (`sprk_communicationthread` null): they never appear in the `by-regarding` threads view (FR-01/FR-03), the participant `participant=` facet still finds them but the record threads view drops them, and Success Criterion 5 ("New messages always land in a sensible thread") fails outright. That is a named contract failure, not "future flexibility."

      **Scope discipline:** extend the resolver's fall-through + add the marker read/write. Do NOT rewrite the Tier-1 JOIN/CREATE, the normalized envelope, the channel key strategies, or the regarding model. Do NOT add a second regarding mechanism (ADR-046/ADR-024) — Tier 2 reuses the SAME `ReadRegardingAnchorAsync` regarding. Do NOT backfill historical messages (point-forward). Do NOT touch the impersonation read path (ADR-028) — this is write/resolve-side only.
    </background>
